WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.672
Look around you. Every material
is a solid, a liquid or a gas.

2
00:00:08.892 --> 00:00:12.840
Ice, water and steam are all
water, in three different states.

3
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.848
Every material is in one of three states.

4
00:00:16.068 --> 00:00:20.665
A solid keeps its own shape.
Ice, wood and a stone are solids.

5
00:00:20.885 --> 00:00:25.297
A liquid flows and takes the shape
of its container. Water and milk.

6
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:30.532
A gas spreads out to fill the
space it is in. Air is a gas.

7
00:00:30.752 --> 00:00:36.186
One material can be in different states.
Ice, water and steam are all water.

8
00:00:36.686 --> 00:00:41.562
A solid keeps its own shape. Turn a
block over and it is still a block.

9
00:00:41.782 --> 00:00:45.544
Tip it as hard as you like. It
does not change shape at all.

10
00:00:45.764 --> 00:00:51.429
A liquid has no shape of its own. Pour
it into a glass and it is glass-shaped.

11
00:00:51.649 --> 00:00:55.643
Pour the same water into a
bowl and it is bowl-shaped.

12
00:00:55.863 --> 00:01:00.972
That is the difference. A solid has
its own shape. A liquid does not.

13
00:01:01.472 --> 00:01:04.955
A gas spreads out to fill
every bit of space it can.

14
00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:09.494
Untie a balloon. The air rushes
out and spreads through the room.

15
00:01:09.714 --> 00:01:14.172
You cannot see the air, but it is
there. Wave your hand and feel it.

16
00:01:14.392 --> 00:01:19.268
Some children think a gas is nothing.
Air is a gas, and it is something.

17
00:01:19.768 --> 00:01:23.437
Ask one thing: does it keep
its shape, flow, or spread out?

18
00:01:23.657 --> 00:01:26.629
Sand pours. Some children
call it a liquid.

19
00:01:26.849 --> 00:01:32.468
Pick up one grain. It keeps its
shape. Sand is lots of tiny solids.

20
00:01:32.688 --> 00:01:36.775
Honey flows slowly, but it
flows. So honey is a liquid.

21
00:01:37.275 --> 00:01:39.550
Last year you measured in hands and cubes.

22
00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.650
But your hand and my
hand are different sizes.

23
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:46.260
So our answers would not agree,
and nobody could check them.

24
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.102
Scientists measure in standard
units, like centimetres.

25
00:01:50.322 --> 00:01:53.712
A centimetre is the same size
for everyone, everywhere.

26
00:01:53.932 --> 00:01:57.647
Count them up the glass: the
water is eight centimetres deep.

27
00:01:58.147 --> 00:02:01.445
Scientists choose the right
equipment for the job.

28
00:02:01.665 --> 00:02:04.358
A ruler measures length, in centimetres.

29
00:02:04.578 --> 00:02:07.922
A measuring jug measures
liquid, in millilitres.

30
00:02:08.142 --> 00:02:12.089
A thermometer measures how
hot. Scales measure how heavy.

31
00:02:12.309 --> 00:02:15.839
A ruler cannot tell you
how much water is in a jug.

32
00:02:16.059 --> 00:02:19.681
So choose the right tool, and
measure in a standard unit.

33
00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:25.382
A solid keeps its shape. A liquid
takes the shape of its container.

34
00:02:25.602 --> 00:02:30.664
A gas spreads out to fill all the
space. Sand is lots of tiny solids.

35
00:02:30.884 --> 00:02:34.228
Measure in standard units, and
choose the right equipment.
